 "drivingQuestion": "Why is she not at least doubtfully betrothed when he may possess the money?",
 "outcome": "inconclusive",
 "outcomeNote": "answered in the next unit (kiddushin.sugya242)",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "kiddushin.60b.14",
   "daf": "60b",
   "sentenceId": 13,
   "he": "״עַל מְנָת שֶׁיֵּשׁ לִי מָאתַיִם זוּז״ וְכוּ׳. וְנֵיחוּשׁ שֶׁמָּא יֵשׁ לוֹ! וְעוֹד, תַּנְיָא: חָיְישִׁינַן שֶׁמָּא יֵשׁ לוֹ!",
   "en": "The mishna further teaches that if he says to her: You are hereby betrothed to me «on the condition that I have two hundred dinars,» if he in fact possesses the specified amount, she is betrothed, but if he does not, she is not betrothed. The Gemara asks: «And let us be concerned» that «perhaps he has» this sum, although he fails to show it to her. How can his betrothal be nullified if there is no conclusive evidence that he does not have the money? «And furthermore, it is taught» in a baraita that «we are concerned» that «perhaps he has» the money."
  }
 ],
